    "lawCode": "Amd §461-e, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8813\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to requiring adult\ncare facilities to include certain demographic information in certain\nrecords maintained by the facility\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo ensure that adult care facilities collect and report race and ethnic-\nity data pertaining to residents and assisted living program partic-\nipants at their facilities.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 5 of section 461-e of the social services\nlaw to include race and ethnicity data among those categories of data\nrequired by regulation pertaining to adult care facilities.\n\nSection 2 provides for the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, adult care facilities (ACFs) in the state are required to\nreport demographic information including the age and sex of residents\nand assisted living program (ALPs) participants on a quarterly basis,\nhowever it is not required that facilities report the race and ethnicity\nof residents who are reigsterd in or discharged from such facility. Data\ncollection is an essential step in identifying racial disparities in\nhealth care, including disparities in access to care, and improving the\nprovision and quality of care and services that ACFs and ALPs offer.\nThis data is already collected from nursing homes through the federal\nMinimum Data Set quarterly reports to CMS, but is not collected from New\nYork's adult care facilities. This bill would require that race and\nethnicity of residents are also reported on by these facilities, recti-\nfying that oversight.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNo significant impact is anticipated.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
